{# Kickoff response prompt — fires ONCE when the frontend agent wakes on a
   ``kickoff_request`` event for milestone M(milestone_index). Single-pass:
   author the `"frontend"` section of the kickoff meeting (ui_pages +
   user_flows + auth + reference_image_manifest + feature_inventory +
   done_def + screens), record it as a meeting decision, then finish().
   The orchestrator (NOT this agent) drives synthesis + RegistryHub
   registration — see runtime/kickoff/run_kickoff.py.

   Canonical contract shape comes from runtime/kickoff/contract.py
   (KickoffEndpoint / KickoffDataModel / KickoffAuth) and the per-section
   shape comment in run_kickoff.py:
     - backend  → api_endpoints (KickoffEndpoint with response_key) + data_model
     - frontend → ui_pages + user_flows + feature_inventory + done_def +
                  auth + screens + reference_image_manifest
     - verifier → predicates

   This agent owns the FRONTEND section — its `content` carries BOTH the
   former design fields (ui_pages, user_flows, auth, etc.) AND the native
   frontend fields (screens). Each screen.user_flow MUST be a flow id you
   author in user_flows; api_calls reference backend endpoints by their
   canonical id `"<METHOD> <path>"` (matches `contract.endpoint_id`). #}
{% macro kickoff_response_prompt(meeting_id, milestone_index, requirements, expected_section) %}
READ THIS FIRST — the binding protocol is the FINAL PROTOCOL block at the END of this prompt (typed declare tools, one call per item; it overrides every inline example in between).
<kickoff_response_briefing>
You are responding to a `kickoff_request` event for **milestone M{{ milestone_index }}** of the meeting `{{ meeting_id }}`.

This is a SINGLE-PASS response. You will:
  1. **INSPECT REFERENCE MATERIAL FIRST (MANDATORY when any is provided).** Call `list_reference_images()` ONCE to enumerate the available reference screenshots, then `view_image(<path>)` for EVERY path it returns — you may NOT author the `ui_pages` / `screens` / `reference_image_manifest` from memory or from the text requirements alone when reference images exist. The whole point of references is fidelity: the visual-fidelity gate will later compare your shipped screens against these exact images, so a section authored without looking at them ships a wrong UI and fails. If references include staged documents (feature write-ups, HTML, PDFs under `design/references/`, compiled into `design/reference_spec.json`), read that spec too. ONLY if `list_reference_images()` returns NOTHING may you proceed from the text requirements — and then say so explicitly in your `reference_image_manifest` (e.g. `{}` with a note "no references provided"). Every `ui_pages[].reference_image` / `reference_image_manifest` entry you author MUST be a path you actually viewed this step.
  2. Read inputs (requirements + prior-milestone history if M{{ milestone_index }} > 1).
  3. Author the **`{{ expected_section }}`** section of the kickoff decision — ground every screen's layout/components/chrome in the reference image you viewed for it, not in a generic guess.
  4. Record it via FILE-FIRST authoring: `write('design/kickoff_frontend_section.json', <your full decision JSON>)` then `workhub_add_meeting_decision(meeting_id=..., decision_file='design/kickoff_frontend_section.json')`. Your section is LARGE — long nested-JSON inline tool arguments are unreliable; the file path keeps the call small. Only use the inline `decision={...}` form for small payloads.
  5. Call `finish()`.

Do NOT poll the meeting for synthesis. The orchestrator (NOT you) reads the decisions, runs the cross-check suite, resolves arbitration, registers endpoints into RegistryHub, and emits `kickoff_complete`. You will wake on the downstream `task_ready` AFTER `kickoff_complete`; that is when normal Phase A / Phase B work begins.

Workspace conventions: read `design/agent_notes.md` (response shapes, auth, file-first authoring, task discipline) before authoring.

**Inputs**
- Requirements (verbatim from orchestrator):
```
{{ requirements }}
```
- Milestone index: {{ milestone_index }}
{% if milestone_index > 1 %}
- Milestone history: read M{{ milestone_index - 1 }} artifacts via `registryhub_list_endpoints(status='implemented')` + `workhub_list_documents()` so this milestone's screens reuse the existing endpoint vocabulary instead of inventing new ones. Do NOT register new endpoints — that is backend's job during synthesis.
{% else %}
- This is the first milestone. No prior history to read.
{% endif %}

**Your section to author: `{{ expected_section }}`**

**Design ownership**: this lane owns design + frontend together. YOU author
the UI design fields (ui_pages / reference_image_manifest / screens) AND the
native frontend fields. You do NOT author ``user_flows`` — end-to-end user-flow
TESTING is defined later by the orchestrator at the verify/fix stage (one flow
= one test task), derived from the registered contract (ui_pages + endpoints);
it is not a kickoff deliverable. ``screens`` need NOT carry a ``user_flow`` id
(it is optional and may be omitted). The ``reference_image_manifest`` is YOUR
responsibility (inspect via ``view_image()`` per the Phase A mandate).

**Cross-section vocabulary you depend on** (don't redefine — sibling sections own these):
- ``backend.api_endpoints[i]`` — each carries ``{method, path, response_key, auth_required, request, response}``. The ``response_key`` is the FIXED projector envelope key: ``"items"`` for a list, ``"item"`` for a single resource (those are the only two values for a business endpoint). The frontend service layer reads ``data.items`` / ``data.item`` accordingly during Phase B. You declare which endpoints you need by canonical ``endpoint_id`` (``"<METHOD> <path>"``); backend authors the full record.
- ``verifier.predicates[].flow`` — every CRITICAL ``user_flow`` you author MUST have at least one verifier predicate. Your screens reference flows, verifier covers them — same flow ids.

The structured content shape for the `frontend` section is:

```python
{
    # --- former design fields (now owned by frontend) ---
    "ui_pages": [
        {
            "id":              str,   # stable kebab-case id, MUST match a screens[i].id below
            "path":            str,   # URL path, e.g. "/login"
            "reference_image": str,   # path under screenshots/ (optional if no image provided)
        },
        ...
    ],
    "user_flows": [
        {
            "id":         str,        # flow id, e.g. "auth_login"
            "critical":   bool,       # True ⇒ verifier MUST cover with a predicate
            "pages":      [str, ...], # ui_pages[].id entries this flow traverses
            "predicates": [str, ...], # placeholder for verifier predicate ids; usually []
        },
        ...
    ],
    "auth": {
        "model":   str,    # e.g. "jwt_bearer", "session_cookie", "none"
        "details": dict,   # free-form; e.g. {"token_storage": "localStorage", "header": "Authorization: Bearer"}
    },
    "reference_image_manifest": {
        # path -> short description
        "screenshots/login.png": "Login page — email + password + Submit",
        ...
    },
    "feature_inventory": {
        # free-form mapping of feature_id -> short description / ownership notes
        # used by cross_check_suite to verify done_def coverage.
    },
    "done_def": [
        # acceptance criteria for the milestone, as a flat list of strings
        # e.g. "User can log in with email+password and see the feed"
        str, ...
    ],
    # --- native frontend fields ---
    "screens": [
        {
            "id":         str,    # stable identifier, kebab-case — MUST also appear in ui_pages[].id
